WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Multitasking often feels productive because it creates visible activity.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.960
Messages are answered, windows are open, meetings are attended, and several tasks appear to move at once.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.180
The hidden cost is that attention keeps resetting.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.600
Context switching forces the mind to reload the goal, current state, constraints, and next decision.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.340
That reset consumes time and mental energy even when it feels quick.

6
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:33.340
Attention residue is the leftover mental attention from a previous task that interferes with the next one.

7
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:41.200
After a difficult message thread, part of the mind may still be rehearsing the conversation while the person is trying to analyze data or review code.

8
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:47.380
Working memory is limited, so residue and active task demands compete for the same mental space.

9
00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:54.540
Cognitive load rises when tasks are complex, ambiguous, unfamiliar, or interrupted.

10
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.680
Under high load, error risk increases.

11
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:06.500
People miss details, repeat work, choose shallow shortcuts, or take longer to finish because the task never receives a clean block of attention.

12
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:13.000
Multitasking can therefore reduce both quality and throughput, even when the person feels busy the entire time.

13
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.240
Not all multitasking is equally expensive.

14
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.580
Listening to familiar music while doing routine cleanup may be low risk.

15
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.760
Monitoring a system dashboard while waiting for a planned event may be reasonable.

16
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:36.540
But multitasking becomes expensive when the work requires judgment, learning, empathy, precision, writing, design, troubleshooting, or a decision with consequences.

17
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.120
The more attention the task needs, the more costly splitting attention becomes.
